\### Licensing/provenance note



A complete C64 BASIC ROM symbol set may be generated from historical address-to-label mappings without reproducing protected commentary or source code.



The relevant BASIC labels were not authored by Sheldon Leemon for \*Mapping the Commodore 64\*; they were taken from earlier Commodore materials, including the \*Commodore 64 Programmer’s Reference Guide\* and Commodore ROM/source listings. Individual routine names and their associations with ROM addresses are factual technical identifiers rather than Leemon-authored prose.



The generated `c64ref-basic.yaml` should therefore contain only:



\* ROM addresses;

\* historical routine identifiers;

\* project-authored normalization or namespace metadata.



It must not reproduce:



\* Leemon’s routine descriptions, examples, headings, or commentary;

\* comments or prose from Commodore source listings;

\* Lee Davison’s disassembly comments;

\* source code or instruction sequences from any reference.



The output will be independently arranged in ascending address order and expressed in the project’s own YAML schema. Names should be cross-checked against multiple historical references where practical, including Commodore documentation, ROM listings, VICE-compatible symbol files, and other independent disassemblies.



A suitable source description is:



> This generated dataset records factual correspondences between C64 BASIC ROM addresses and historically documented routine identifiers. It does not incorporate source code, explanatory prose, comments, or other expressive material from the consulted references.



Accordingly, a Leemon-derived machine-readable symbol file is not automatically encumbered merely because it reproduces historical Commodore labels. The remaining implementation task is to distinguish inherited Commodore identifiers from any editorial labels added by later authors, import only address/name pairs, and document the factual-extraction and verification process.
